money": 0.56}, "rating_margin": 7.0, "sigma_race": 5.0, "sigma_total": 5.72, "baseline_margin": 8.92, "analyst_adjustment": -0.12, "key_factors": ["Cooper leads every recent poll by 5-15 points, averaging near 10", "Senate Leadership Fund and other GOP super PACs canceled or paused NC advertising in early October", "Cooper's fundraising edge: $35M raised and $21M cash versus Whatley's $11M and $3.5M", "Strong midterm environment for the out-party offsets North Carolina's slight Republican lean"], "rationale": "Cooper leads every recent poll by five to fifteen points, holds a roughly three-to-one fundraising advantage, and benefits as major Republican outside groups pull their spending a month before Election Day; the state's slight Republican lean and normal polling uncertainty keep this short of safe.", "watch": "Renewed Republican outside spending or a late shift in the national midterm environment could narrow the margin.", "confidence": "high", "notes": ["Open seat: Thom Tillis (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "North Carolina's Senate race is an open-seat contest between Democrat Roy Cooper and Republican Michael Whatley, with incumbent Thom Tillis retiring. Cooper leads every recent poll by margins ranging from five to fifteen points, averaging near ten ahead. Even Republican-sponsored surveys show him in front. Expert raters across the spectrum call the race a Democratic-leaning flip, and prediction markets put Cooper's odds in the mid-nineties.\n\nMoney and outside signals reinforce the polling picture. Cooper holds a roughly six-to-one cash advantage, and outside groups have spent far more attacking Whatley than Cooper. In early October, Senate Leadership Fund and other Republican super PACs canceled or paused advertising in the state, redirecting money elsewhere and signaling fading GOP confidence. North Carolina leans slightly Republican in fundamentals, but the midterm environment and Cooper's consistent polling edge point toward a Democratic pic